#!/usr/bin/env python3

"""
Run smoke tests - basic use-case checks with default data

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoke_testing_(software)

Usage:

    ./dev/run-smoke-tests <path_to_treetime_executable> [--only-failures] [--no-stats] [--output-dir=PATH] [--manifest=PATH]

Options:
    --only-failures    Show only failed tests
    --no-stats         Omit performance statistics
    --output-dir=PATH  Write command outputs below PATH instead of tmp/smoke-tests
    --manifest=PATH    Write a JSON completion manifest to PATH

The manifest is a single JSON object written atomically only after the whole matrix has run:

    {schema, completed: true, generated_at, binary, binary_sha256, results_dir,
     matrix_id, input_id, case_count, cases: [{id, cmd_name, data_name, code, output_dir}, ...]}

Because it is written once, at the end, via a temp-file rename, its presence attests that the driver
finished the matrix (with or without per-case failures); a driver that dies mid-run leaves no fresh
manifest. `binary_sha256`, `matrix_id`, and `input_id` bind the run to the exact binary, test matrix,
and input files it covered. `dev/smoke-compare` consumes these fields to reject stale or mismatched
evidence.

NOTE: you must build and re-build the tested executable yourself, this script does not do that

For a before/after output-equality comparison across two binaries, use `./dev/smoke-compare`, which
drives this script twice (persisting the "before" run) and diffs every non-failing test's outputs.


How to add more tests:

1. Add ancestral commands by adding entries to `ANCESTRAL_BATCH_RUNS`
2. Add clock commands by adding entries to `CLOCK_BATCH_RUNS`
3. Add mugration commands by adding entries to `MUGRATION_BATCH_RUNS`
4. Add arbitrary commands to `make_additional_commands()`

"""

